But I'm not doing that. You're the one who's saying any colour a
particular person might like to suppose is red is red, and that definition
becomes meaningless.

>If you insist that the only 
>true roleplaying games are those whose rules are whatever it is that 
>you assert makes something a roleplaying game, that's also meaningless.  

No. Meaningless is where you suggest a property which is true of any game;
in this case, that one might identify with some entity in play. By your
definition, chess is a roleplaying game; you might identify with a knight.
By your definition, "roleplaying game" conveys nothing more than "game"
because there is no game in which one might not identify with something.
That's meaningless, because you have rendered the adjective completely
useless.

Usage always finds it hard to fight against compound words that say what
they mean. There's always going to be a tendency to notice that
"roleplaying game" can only reasonably be taken to mean "a game where you
play a role".

Furthermore, you're switching horses with the argument from majority
usage, because the majority usage is probably "game with mechanics
reminiscent of D&D". That's got nothing to do with identifying with @
signs.
